      {
        "id": "post-qft-regulated-polynomial-model",
        "kind": "postulate",
        "domain": "physics",
        "library": "physics",
        "title": "Adopted finite-regulator bosonic polynomial model",
        "reference": "items/post-qft-regulated-polynomial-model.md#Postulate",
        "design_state": "Complete adopted finite-regulator data and scope. The assigned C01 unit-definition supplier has no Step 1 readiness record, so this item is escalated; the QFT-IR01 polynomial definition is current-ready.",
        "dependency_level": 1,
        "deps": [
          "def-countable-choice",
          "def-qft-framework-units",
          "def-qft-regulated-polynomial-hamiltonian"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-qft-framework-units": "formulation-prerequisite",
          "def-qft-regulated-polynomial-hamiltonian": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "inventory_expansion_required": false,
        "statement": "Assume Countable Choice as in [[def-qft-regulated-polynomial-hamiltonian]];\nit supplies the stated Hilbert-space construction and is not a physical\nmeasurement assumption. Fix one finite nonempty label set $J$ with\n$N:=|J|\\ge1$, an enumeration of\n$J$ by $\\{1,\\ldots,N\\}$, and dimensionless real coordinates\n$q=(q_1,\\ldots,q_N)\\in\\mathbb R^N$. The labels may represent sites of a\nfixed finite spatial lattice or a fixed finite set of modes; any graph,\ngeometry, and couplings are additional fixed regulator data. The coordinate\nspace, Hilbert space, and polynomial differential expression are those of\n[[def-qft-regulated-polynomial-hamiltonian]]:\n$H_N=L^2(\\mathbb R^N,dq;\\mathbb C)$, with inner product linear in its first\nvariable, $D_0=C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R^N;\\mathbb C)$, and\n\n$$h_0u=-\\sum_{j=1}^N a_j\\partial_j^2u+V(q)u,\\qquad u\\in D_0,$$\n\nwhere $a_j>0$ and $V$ is a real polynomial. Require fixed constants $c>0$ and\n$C_0\\ge0$ such that\n\n$$V(q)\\ge c|q|^2-C_0\\qquad(q\\in\\mathbb R^N).$$\n\nFor this physical model, take $q_j$, $a_j$, $c$, $C_0$, and the coefficients\nof $V$ to be dimensionless. Adopt a positive energy scale $E_*$ in joules as\nprimitive finite-regulator data, and use $\\hbar$ in joule-seconds under\n[[def-qft-framework-units]]. When the mathematical supplier\n[[thm-qft-self-adjoint-regulated-interaction]] constructs its specified\nself-adjoint form realization $h$ of $h_0$, the model Hamiltonian is\n$H_{\\rm phys}:=E_*h$ on that supplied domain. This postulate adopts the\nfinite-regulator polynomial data; it does not assert that the initial\ndifferential expression is self-adjoint or choose an operator domain by\nitself.\n\nThe finite label set and polynomial are held fixed. A finite spatial regulator\nis not thereby Lorentz invariant, and this model asserts no continuum or\ninfinite-volume limit, no convergence of a perturbation series, and no\nreported observation. Its parameters are selected model inputs, not measured\nvalues.",
        "strategy": "Adopted physical assumption; source and scope reviewed in the item contract.",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "not-applicable"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "David Tong, Quantum Field Theory, author-hosted lecture notes, Chapter 3, Interacting Fields, especially the real-scalar polynomial interaction",
              "url": "https://www.damtp.cam.ac.uk/user/tong/qft/qfthtml/S3.html",
              "locator": "Complete Chapter 3 HTML; scalar polynomial interactions and the explicit phi-four model."
            },
            {
              "title": "Gerald Teschl, Mathematical Methods in Quantum Mechanics, 2nd ed., §2.3",
              "url": "https://www.mat.univie.ac.at/~gerald/ftp/book-schroe/schroe2.pdf",
              "locator": "Printed pp.76–83, quadratic forms and self-adjoint realizations."
            }
          ]
        },
        "physical_scope": "One fixed finite set of modes or sites with a real coercive polynomial potential. The energy scale is a primitive model input. No continuum, infinite-volume, Lorentz-invariance, or empirical-measurement claim is made.",
        "empirical_premises": []
      },
      {
        "id": "def-qft-regulated-thermal-parameter",
        "kind": "definition",
        "domain": "physics",
        "library": "physics",
        "title": "Finite-regulator temperature and inverse-energy parameter",
        "reference": "items/def-qft-regulated-thermal-parameter.md#Definition",
        "design_state": "Complete local temperature and inverse-energy definition. Its framework-units supplier lacks a Step 1 readiness record, and its model postulate is escalated; this item is escalated.",
        "dependency_level": 2,
        "deps": [
          "def-qft-framework-units",
          "post-qft-regulated-polynomial-model"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-qft-framework-units": "formulation-prerequisite",
          "post-qft-regulated-polynomial-model": "physical-assumption"
        },
        "inventory_expansion_required": true,
        "statement": "For the fixed model of [[post-qft-regulated-polynomial-model]], a declared\ntemperature input is a scalar $T>0$ with unit kelvin (K). Take $k_B>0$, with\nunit $\\mathrm{J\\,K^{-1}}$, as the fixed Boltzmann conversion constant in this\nbranch. Define the physical inverse-energy parameter\n\n$$\\beta_{\\rm phys}:=\\frac{1}{k_BT}\\quad[\\mathrm{J^{-1}}],$$\n\nand its dimensionless form\n\n$$\\widehat\\beta:=E_*\\beta_{\\rm phys}.$$\n\nThe product $k_BT$ has unit joule, and $\\widehat\\beta$ is dimensionless\nbecause $E_*$ has unit joule. Since $k_B>0$ and $T>0$, both inverse parameters\nare defined and positive. The values of $T$ and the model's choice of $k_B$\nare inputs; this definition does not infer them from a state or an\nexperiment. It defines a parameter convention only and asserts no dynamical\nthermalization.",
        "strategy": "Definition; parameter domains and positive-denominator well-definedness stated locally.",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "not-applicable"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "David Tong, Statistical Physics, author-hosted lecture notes, Chapter 1, §§1.3–1.3.1, canonical ensemble and density matrix",
              "url": "https://www.damtp.cam.ac.uk/user/tong/statphys/statmechhtml/S1.html",
              "locator": "Complete Chapter 1 HTML; beta=1/(k_B T), canonical density matrix, and partition function."
            }
          ]
        },
        "physical_scope": "A declared positive temperature input for one fixed finite-regulator model; it does not assert thermalization, an observed temperature, or an infinite-volume state.",
        "empirical_premises": []
      },
      {
        "id": "pthm-qft-regulated-interacting-model-evolution",
        "kind": "physics-theorem",
        "domain": "physics",
        "library": "physics",
        "title": "Constructed regulated model evolution",
        "reference": "items/pthm-qft-regulated-interacting-model-evolution.md#Statement",
        "design_state": "Complete conditional proof and SI time conversion. The QFT-IR01 mathematical suppliers are current-ready, but the selected-model postulate and C01 constructed-model postulate lack Step 1 readiness records; this item is escalated.",
        "dependency_level": 6,
        "deps": [
          "def-countable-choice",
          "post-qft-constructed-model-and-measurement",
          "post-qft-regulated-polynomial-model",
          "thm-qft-regulated-global-unitary-evolution",
          "thm-qft-self-adjoint-regulated-interaction"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise",
          "post-qft-constructed-model-and-measurement": "physical-assumption",
          "post-qft-regulated-polynomial-model": "physical-assumption",
          "thm-qft-regulated-global-unitary-evolution": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-qft-self-adjoint-regulated-interaction": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "inventory_expansion_required": false,
        "statement": "Assume Countable Choice. Use one fixed finite-regulator data set from\n[[post-qft-regulated-polynomial-model]], and let $h$ be the self-adjoint form\nrealization supplied by\n[[thm-qft-self-adjoint-regulated-interaction]], with domain $D(h)$ and\neigenpairs $(\\psi_n,\\lambda_n)$. Let $E_*>0$ joules and set\n$H_{\\rm phys}:=E_*h$ on $D(h)$. For physical time $\\tau$ in seconds define\n\n$$U_{\\rm phys}(\\tau):=U(E_*\\tau/\\hbar),$$\n\nwhere $U(s)$ is the dimensionless-parameter group of\n[[thm-qft-regulated-global-unitary-evolution]]. Then $U_{\\rm phys}$ is a\nstrongly continuous unitary group, $U_{\\rm phys}(0)=I$,\n$U_{\\rm phys}(\\tau_1+\\tau_2)=U_{\\rm phys}(\\tau_1)U_{\\rm phys}(\\tau_2)$,\nand $\\|U_{\\rm phys}(\\tau)\\psi\\|=\\|\\psi\\|$ for every $\\psi\\in H_N$.\nIts norm derivative exists exactly for $\\psi\\in D(h)$, and then\n\n$$\\frac{d}{d\\tau}U_{\\rm phys}(\\tau)\\psi\n=-\\frac{i}{\\hbar}H_{\\rm phys}U_{\\rm phys}(\\tau)\\psi.$$\n\nThe result is a conditional deduction for the selected finite-regulator\nmodel. It is not an empirical observation or a continuum-limit theorem.",
        "strategy": "**Proof technique:** direct.\n\n1.1 By [F1], $h$ is self-adjoint on $D(h)$. Multiplication by the positive\nreal scalar $E_*$ preserves self-adjointness and the domain, so\n$H_{\\rm phys}=E_*h$ is self-adjoint on $D(h)$ with eigenvalues\n$E_*\\lambda_n$. The units in [A2] make $E_*\\tau/\\hbar$ dimensionless.\n[A2, F1, algebra]\n\n2.1 Put $s=E_*\\tau/\\hbar$. Since this is a real linear change of parameter,\n[F2] gives unitarity, strong continuity, the identity at zero, and the group\nlaw for $U_{\\rm phys}(\\tau)=U(s)$. Unitarity also gives\n$\\|U_{\\rm phys}(\\tau)\\psi\\|=\\|\\psi\\|$. [F2, step 1.1, algebra]\n\n3.1 If $\\psi\\in D(h)$, the chain rule and [F2] give\n$\\frac{d}{d\\tau}U(E_*\\tau/\\hbar)\\psi\n=-i(E_*/\\hbar)hU(E_*\\tau/\\hbar)\\psi\n=-iH_{\\rm phys}U_{\\rm phys}(\\tau)\\psi/\\hbar$.\nConversely, because $E_*/\\hbar>0$, the change $s=E_*\\tau/\\hbar$ is\ninvertible, so norm differentiability in $\\tau$ is equivalent to\ndifferentiability in $s$; [F2] makes its domain exactly $D(h)$.\n[F1, F2, step 1.1, step 2.1, algebra]\n\n4.1 [A3] supplies the physical reading of this already constructed\nself-adjoint Hamiltonian and group as the selected model's Schrödinger\nevolution. It adds no existence claim beyond [F1] and [F2], and no empirical\npremise is used. [A3, step 1.1, step 2.1] ∎",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "ai-altered",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Gerald Teschl, Mathematical Methods in Quantum Mechanics, 2nd ed., §5.1, the time evolution and Stone's theorem",
              "url": "https://www.mat.univie.ac.at/~gerald/ftp/book-schroe/schroe2.pdf",
              "locator": "Printed pp.145–149, strongly continuous unitary groups and differentiability on the generator domain."
            },
            {
              "title": "Richard S. Laugesen, Spectral Theory of Partial Differential Equations — Lecture Notes, Ch.4 and Ch.8",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/1203.2344v1",
              "locator": "Printed pp.27–32 and 48–50, compact-form spectral theory and confining Schrödinger operators."
            }
          ]
        },
        "physical_scope": "Global Schrödinger evolution at one fixed finite regulator, with the form-selected unbounded Hamiltonian and its exact domain. No continuum or empirical claim.",
        "empirical_premises": []
      },
      {
        "id": "post-qft-regulated-equilibrium-state",
        "kind": "postulate",
        "domain": "physics",
        "library": "physics",
        "title": "Adopted finite-regulator equilibrium state",
        "reference": "items/post-qft-regulated-equilibrium-state.md#Postulate",
        "design_state": "Complete adopted positive-temperature state assignment with the QFT-IR01 trace bound as its well-definedness input. The QFT-IR01 trace theorem is current-ready, but the local model postulate is escalated; this item is escalated.",
        "dependency_level": 6,
        "deps": [
          "def-countable-choice",
          "def-qft-regulated-thermal-parameter",
          "post-qft-regulated-polynomial-model",
          "thm-qft-regulated-gibbs-trace"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-qft-regulated-thermal-parameter": "formulation-prerequisite",
          "post-qft-regulated-polynomial-model": "physical-assumption",
          "thm-qft-regulated-gibbs-trace": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "inventory_expansion_required": false,
        "statement": "Assume Countable Choice ([[def-countable-choice]]) for the finite-regulator\nspectral and trace construction. For the fixed model of\n[[post-qft-regulated-polynomial-model]] and a declared\ntemperature input $T>0$, use\n[[def-qft-regulated-thermal-parameter]] to set\n$\\beta_{\\rm phys}=(k_BT)^{-1}$ and\n$\\widehat\\beta=E_*\\beta_{\\rm phys}>0$. Let $h$ be the model's specified\nself-adjoint form realization and $H_{\\rm phys}=E_*h$. When the finite-mode\ncoercivity hypotheses hold, [[thm-qft-regulated-gibbs-trace]] proves that\n$e^{-\\widehat\\beta h}$ is positive trace class with finite positive trace.\nAdopt the normalized operator\n\n$$\\rho_T:=\\frac{e^{-\\beta_{\\rm phys}H_{\\rm phys}}}\n{Z_T},\\qquad\nZ_T:=\\operatorname{Tr}(e^{-\\beta_{\\rm phys}H_{\\rm phys}})$$\n\nas this finite-regulator model's canonical equilibrium state. This is a\nphysical state assignment, not a deduction that the dynamics reach\nequilibrium. It asserts no infinite-volume trace, no zero-temperature state,\nand no empirical observation.",
        "strategy": "Adopted physical assumption; source and scope reviewed in the item contract.",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "not-applicable"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "David Tong, Statistical Physics, author-hosted lecture notes, Chapter 1, §§1.3–1.3.1, canonical ensemble and density matrix",
              "url": "https://www.damtp.cam.ac.uk/user/tong/statphys/statmechhtml/S1.html",
              "locator": "Complete Chapter 1 HTML; canonical beta, density matrix, and partition function."
            },
            {
              "title": "Gerald Teschl, Mathematical Methods in Quantum Mechanics, 2nd ed., §6.3",
              "url": "https://www.mat.univie.ac.at/~gerald/ftp/book-schroe/schroe2.pdf",
              "locator": "Printed pp.163–169, trace-class operators and basis-independent trace."
            }
          ]
        },
        "physical_scope": "A canonical finite-temperature state for one fixed finite regulator with T>0 and finite Gibbs trace. No thermalization dynamics, empirical temperature measurement, infinite-volume trace, or zero-temperature limit is asserted.",
        "empirical_premises": []
      },
      {
        "id": "pthm-qft-regulated-equilibrium-predictions",
        "kind": "physics-theorem",
        "domain": "physics",
        "library": "physics",
        "title": "Finite-regulator interacting Gibbs predictions",
        "reference": "items/pthm-qft-regulated-equilibrium-predictions.md#Statement",
        "design_state": "Complete conditional proof with physical inverse temperature rescaled by E_* before applying the finite mathematical trace theorem. The QFT-IR01 trace input is current-ready, but the local equilibrium-state postulate and C01 constructed-model postulate lack current readiness; this item is escalated.",
        "dependency_level": 7,
        "deps": [
          "def-countable-choice",
          "def-qft-regulated-thermal-parameter",
          "post-qft-constructed-model-and-measurement",
          "post-qft-regulated-equilibrium-state",
          "thm-qft-regulated-gibbs-trace"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-qft-regulated-thermal-parameter": "formulation-prerequisite",
          "post-qft-constructed-model-and-measurement": "physical-assumption",
          "post-qft-regulated-equilibrium-state": "physical-assumption",
          "thm-qft-regulated-gibbs-trace": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "inventory_expansion_required": false,
        "statement": "Assume Countable Choice and the finite-regulator coercivity hypotheses of\n[[thm-qft-regulated-gibbs-trace]]. Use the model state of\n[[post-qft-regulated-equilibrium-state]] and the inverse-energy parameters of\n[[def-qft-regulated-thermal-parameter]]. Let\n$\\alpha_j:=\\sqrt{a_jc}$, $j=1,\\ldots,N$, for the dimensionless kinetic\ncoefficients and coercivity constant in the model, and let\n$C_0\\ge0$ be its potential lower-bound shift. Then\n\n$$0<Z_T=\\sum_{k\\ge0}e^{-\\widehat\\beta\\lambda_k}\n\\le e^{\\widehat\\beta C_0}\n\\prod_{j=1}^N\n\\frac{e^{-\\widehat\\beta\\alpha_j}}\n{1-e^{-2\\widehat\\beta\\alpha_j}}<\\infty,$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda_k$ are the eigenvalues of $h$. The adopted state\n$\\rho_T$ is a positive trace-class operator with trace one. For every\nbounded linear operator $B:H_N\\to H_N$,\n\n$$\\operatorname{Tr}(\\rho_TB)\n=Z_T^{-1}\\sum_{k\\ge0}e^{-\\widehat\\beta\\lambda_k}\n\\langle B\\psi_k,\\psi_k\\rangle,$$\n\nand the series is absolutely convergent. If $E_a$ is an effect in a supplied\nnormalized finite instrument of\n[[post-qft-constructed-model-and-measurement]], this trace is its adopted\ncanonical model probability. These are conditional finite-regulator\npredictions, not measured outcomes or a continuum-limit claim.",
        "strategy": "**Proof technique:** direct.\n\n1.1 By [F2], $\\widehat\\beta=E_*\\beta_{\\rm phys}>0$ is dimensionless.\nThe eigenvalues of $H_{\\rm phys}=E_*h$ are $E_*\\lambda_k$, so its Gibbs\nweights are\n$e^{-\\beta_{\\rm phys}E_*\\lambda_k}\n=e^{-\\widehat\\beta\\lambda_k}$. Therefore\n$Z_T=\\sum_ke^{-\\widehat\\beta\\lambda_k}$. [A2, F2, given, algebra]\n\n2.1 Apply [F1] at $\\widehat\\beta>0$. It gives $Z_T>0$, the stated finite\nupper bound, and trace-class positivity of $e^{-\\widehat\\beta h}$.\nDividing by $Z_T$ yields $\\operatorname{Tr}(\\rho_T)=1$ and preserves\npositivity and trace class. [A1, A2, F1, step 1.1, algebra]\n\n3.1 For bounded $B$, [F1] gives the absolutely convergent sum for\n$\\operatorname{Tr}(e^{-\\widehat\\beta h}B)$. Since\n$\\rho_T=Z_T^{-1}e^{-\\widehat\\beta h}$, linearity of the trace and\n$Z_T>0$ give the displayed normalized formula. If $B=E_a$ is an effect in\nthe instrument of [A3], that postulate supplies its physical model\nprobability interpretation; the finite trace calculation supplies its\nvalue. [A2, A3, F1, step 2.1, algebra] ∎",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "ai-altered",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Gerald Teschl, Mathematical Methods in Quantum Mechanics, 2nd ed., §6.3",
              "url": "https://www.mat.univie.ac.at/~gerald/ftp/book-schroe/schroe2.pdf",
              "locator": "Printed pp.163–169, trace-class operators and basis-independent trace."
            },
            {
              "title": "Richard S. Laugesen, Spectral Theory of Partial Differential Equations — Lecture Notes, Ch.8 and Ch.9",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/1203.2344v1",
              "locator": "Printed pp.48–54, confining-potential eigenvalues and variational characterization."
            },
            {
              "title": "David Tong, Statistical Physics, author-hosted lecture notes, Chapter 1, §§1.3–1.3.1",
              "url": "https://www.damtp.cam.ac.uk/user/tong/statphys/statmechhtml/S1.html",
              "locator": "Complete Chapter 1 HTML; canonical density matrix and trace partition function."
            }
          ]
        },
        "physical_scope": "Trace-normalized bounded-observable expectations at T>0 for one fixed finite regulator. No infinite-volume or continuum trace, empirical data, or unbounded-observable formula is asserted.",
        "empirical_premises": []
      },
      {
        "id": "pthm-qft-quartic-model-energy-bound",
        "kind": "physics-theorem",
        "domain": "physics",
        "library": "physics",
        "title": "Controlled quartic-model ground-energy bound",
        "reference": "items/pthm-qft-quartic-model-energy-bound.md#Statement",
        "design_state": "Complete conditional form comparison and Gaussian trial-state proof. Its QFT-IR01 mathematical suppliers are current-ready, but the selected-model and C01 constructed-model postulates lack current Step 1 readiness records; this item is escalated.",
        "dependency_level": 6,
        "deps": [
          "def-countable-choice",
          "lem-qft-coercive-polynomial-form-domain",
          "lem-qft-hermite-oscillator-research-supplier",
          "lem-qft-regulated-ground-variational-bound",
          "post-qft-constructed-model-and-measurement",
          "post-qft-regulated-polynomial-model",
          "thm-qft-self-adjoint-regulated-interaction"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise",
          "lem-qft-coercive-polynomial-form-domain": "mathematical-premise",
          "lem-qft-hermite-oscillator-research-supplier": "mathematical-premise",
          "lem-qft-regulated-ground-variational-bound": "mathematical-premise",
          "post-qft-constructed-model-and-measurement": "physical-assumption",
          "post-qft-regulated-polynomial-model": "physical-assumption",
          "thm-qft-self-adjoint-regulated-interaction": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "inventory_expansion_required": false,
        "statement": "Assume Countable Choice and use one fixed one-mode regulator with\ndimensionless $a>0$, $r>0$, and $\\lambda\\ge0$. Here $r$ is a polynomial\ncoefficient, not an SI rest mass. Take\n$V_\\lambda(q)=\\frac{r^2}{2}q^2+\\lambda q^4$ on\n$H_1=L^2(\\mathbb R,dq;\\mathbb C)$, with the form realization from\n[[thm-qft-self-adjoint-regulated-interaction]]. Let $\\epsilon_g(\\lambda)$\nbe its least eigenvalue. The physical ground-energy prediction for the\nadopted scale $E_*>0$ joules is\n$E_g^{\\rm phys}(\\lambda):=E_*\\epsilon_g(\\lambda)$. Then\n\n$$0\\le E_g^{\\rm phys}(\\lambda)-E_g^{\\rm phys}(0)\n\\le \\frac{3E_*a\\lambda}{2r^2}.$$\n\nFor $\\lambda>0$ this is an interacting one-mode model on the full\n$L^2(\\mathbb R)$ space. The inequality is an exact fixed-regulator model\ndeduction; it supplies neither an observed energy nor a perturbation-series\nor continuum-limit claim.",
        "strategy": "**Proof technique:** direct.\n\n1.1 Since $V_\\lambda(q)=r^2q^2/2+\\lambda q^4\\ge (r^2/2)q^2$, the\ncoercivity hypotheses of [F1] hold with $c=r^2/2$ and $C_0=0$. Thus the\nforms $q_\\lambda$ and $q_0$ have the stated form realizations.\n[A1, F1, given, algebra]\n\n2.1 On $C_c^\\infty$, the shifted form norms obey\n$\\|u\\|_{Q_0}^2\\le\\|u\\|_{Q_\\lambda}^2$ because their difference is\n$\\lambda\\int q^4|u|^2dq\\ge0$. Hence the identity extends continuously\n$Q_\\lambda\\to Q_0$, and the two continuous injections into $L^2$ identify\nthe same representative. On the core,\n$q_\\lambda(u)=q_0(u)+\\lambda\\int q^4|u|^2dq\\ge q_0(u)$; approximating any\n$u\\in Q_\\lambda$ by core elements and using continuity of both forms passes\nthis inequality to $u$. For a normalized $u$, [F3] gives\n$q_0(u)\\ge\\epsilon_g(0)$, so taking the infimum on $Q_\\lambda$ yields\n$\\epsilon_g(\\lambda)\\ge\\epsilon_g(0)$. [F1, F3, step 1.1, algebra]\n\n2.2 Let $g_b(q)=(b/\\pi)^{1/4}e^{-bq^2/2}$ with $b=r/\\sqrt{2a}$, the\nnormalized ground function in [F2] for $c=r^2/2$. Choose the smooth cutoff\n$\\chi_R(q)=\\chi(q/R)$ from [F2], with $\\chi=1$ on $[-1,1]$,\n$\\chi=0$ outside $[-2,2]$, and $|\\chi_R'|\\le C/R$. Then\n$u_R=\\chi_Rg_b\\in C_c^\\infty$. For $S>R$, $u_R-u_S$ is supported where\n$|q|\\ge R$, and\n$\\|(u_R-u_S)'\\|_2\\le\\|1_{|q|\\ge R}g_b'\\|_2\n+C(R^{-1}+S^{-1})\\|1_{|q|\\ge R}g_b\\|_2\\to0$.\nThe weighted part of its $Q_\\lambda$ norm is bounded in square by\n$\\int_{|q|\\ge R}(r^2q^2/2+\\lambda q^4+2)|g_b(q)|^2dq\\to0$.\nThese tails vanish because each polynomial times $e^{-bq^2}$ is integrable:\nfor large $|q|$, its product with $|q|^2$ is bounded. Thus $(u_R)$ is\n$Q_\\lambda$-Cauchy. Its $L^2$ limit is $g_b$, so injectivity in [F1] gives\n$g_b\\in Q_\\lambda$. [F1, F2, step 1.1, construct, algebra]\n\n3.1 From $g_b'=-bqg_b$ and $\\|g_b\\|_2=1$, integrate the derivatives of\n$q e^{-bq^2}$ and $q^3e^{-bq^2}$ over $\\mathbb R$. Their boundary terms\nvanish by Gaussian decay, giving\n$\\int q^2|g_b|^2dq=1/(2b)$ and\n$\\int q^4|g_b|^2dq=3/(4b^2)$. Hence\n$q_\\lambda(g_b)=ab/2+r^2/(4b)+3\\lambda/(4b^2)$. At\n$b=r/\\sqrt{2a}$, the first two terms equal\n$\\epsilon_g(0)=r\\sqrt{a/2}$ by [F2], and the last term is\n$3a\\lambda/(2r^2)$. [F2, step 2.2, algebra]\n\n4.1 Since $g_b\\in Q_\\lambda$ is normalized, [F3] gives\n$\\epsilon_g(\\lambda)\\le q_\\lambda(g_b)$. Combining this with step 2.1 and\nstep 3.1 yields\n$0\\le\\epsilon_g(\\lambda)-\\epsilon_g(0)\\le3a\\lambda/(2r^2)$.\n[F3, step 2.1, step 3.1, algebra]\n\n5.1 Multiplication by $E_*>0$ preserves both inequalities and gives the\nstated joule-valued bound. By [A3], this is a selected-model energy\nprediction and not a reported measurement. [A2, A3, step 4.1, algebra] ∎",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "ai-altered",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Gerald Teschl, Mathematical Methods in Quantum Mechanics, 2nd ed., §§4.4 and 8.3",
              "url": "https://www.mat.univie.ac.at/~gerald/ftp/book-schroe/schroe2.pdf",
              "locator": "Printed pp.139–141 and 212–214, Rayleigh–Ritz and the oscillator ground state."
            },
            {
              "title": "Richard S. Laugesen, Spectral Theory of Partial Differential Equations — Lecture Notes, Chs.8–9",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/1203.2344v1",
              "locator": "Printed pp.48–54, confining potentials and variational eigenvalue characterizations."
            },
            {
              "title": "David Tong, Quantum Field Theory, author-hosted lecture notes, Chapter 3, Interacting Fields",
              "url": "https://www.damtp.cam.ac.uk/user/tong/qft/qfthtml/S3.html",
              "locator": "Complete Chapter 3 HTML, real-scalar phi-four interaction and perturbative-scope discussion."
            }
