        "inventory_expansion_required": false,
        "statement": "Choose $N=1$, $a=1$, $r=1$, and $\\lambda=1$, all dimensionless. The\npotential is $V(q)=q^2/2+q^4$, and the physical Hamiltonian is $H_{\\rm phys}\n=E_*h$ with $E_*>0$ joules. The one-mode space is the full\n$H_1=L^2(\\mathbb R,dq;\\mathbb C)$ of\n[[def-qft-regulated-polynomial-hamiltonian]], not a finite matrix truncation.\nThe positive $q^4$ term is an actual polynomial interaction in this selected\nfinite regulator.",
        "strategy": "1.1 The parameters satisfy $a>0$, $r>0$, and $\\lambda>0$, and\n$V(q)=q^2/2+q^4\\ge q^2/2$, so the coercivity hypotheses hold with\n$c=1/2$ and $C_0=0$. The coordinate remains any real $q$ in\n$L^2(\\mathbb R)$; the characteristic functions\n$\\mathbf 1_{[3n,3n+1)}$, $n\\in\\mathbb N$, are nonzero pairwise\northogonal vectors, so this Hilbert space is infinite-dimensional.\n[F3, algebra]\n\n2.1 Substituting $a=r=\\lambda=1$ into [F2] gives\n$0\\le E_g^{\\rm phys}(1)-E_g^{\\rm phys}(0)\\le3E_*/2$.\nThis is an exact bound for the selected model, not an exact value of either\nground energy. [F2, step 1.1, algebra]\n\n3.1 Applying [F1] to the same selected model gives a global unitary group\n$U_{\\rm phys}(\\tau)$ for every $\\tau\\in\\mathbb R$, norm preservation for\nall states, and differentiability precisely on the stated Hamiltonian\ndomain. The quartic coefficient is positive, and the Hilbert space is\ninfinite-dimensional by step 1.1; thus this is an interacting operator model,\nnot a finite matrix replacement. No observation or continuum extrapolation\nis asserted. [F1, step 1.1, algebra] ∎",
